```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Multi-turn agentic LLM serving (e.g., assistant platforms with tool-calling) where each turn appends USER+ASSISTANT context (COMMITTED, reused by later turns) and a one-shot TOOL-RESULT payload (TRANSIENT, never re-referenced). Operators running vLLM with enable_prefix_caching under cache pressure who can signal tool-boundary metadata would see fewer recomputed prefill tokens.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only pending GPU validation. L0 sim shows strict Pareto dominance over LRU and pin-shared-prefix at every cap_frac and shared_prefix_frac (pooled Δhit +0.0124 to +0.1295, 95% CI excludes 0; Δrecompute −274 to −2,850) and the win is NOT trivial pinning in 19/20 cells (pin captures 0% of gap). However the robust regime is cap 0.5–1.5×; at cap=0.25 ∧ sf=0.90 the win collapses to ~trivial shared-prefix pinning (pin captures 99.9%), and under deep starvation naive2 marginally out-hits adaptive3 (−0.0037 @0.25). The sim uses content-addressed proxy tokens, no GPU, no PagedAttention overhead, no TTFT/throughput measurement, and the absolute gain is +2.3pp (cap=1.0) to +3.0pp (cap=1.5) hit-rate — modest vs. a paged kernel that already keeps hot prefixes resident via LRU. Candidate-grade for real vLLM on devgpu014/H100 is explicitly owed; without it the advantage is a sim artifact.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Requires vLLM (or equivalent) code change to track per-block COMMITTED vs TRANSIENT class and implement 3-class eviction priority; Agent framework must emit reliable tool-boundary signals to classify payloads, adding integration surface and risk of misclassification; Adds metadata and eviction-path overhead to the hot prefix-cache path; Gain is 0.4–3.0pp hit-rate (hundreds of tokens) — may not justify complexity vs. existing LRU + pin-shared-prefix knobs already available in SGLang/RadixAttention and TensorRT-LLM; Prior-art sweep still owed — if SGLang/TRT-LLM priority retention already expresses “evict transient first” via config, the policy is not novel and adoption is moot; Operational corner case (deep starvation + high shared prefix) reduces to trivial pinning, limiting value for memory-constrained deployments.]
RATIONALE: Packet reports “adaptive3 strictly Pareto-dominates BOTH required baselines (LRU and pin-shared-prefix) at every cap_frac” with “all Δhit positive with 95% CI excluding 0; all Δrecompute negative with CI excluding 0” (EXP-0002 Results). Disaggregated, “pin == LRU exactly” in 19/20 cells, so “the 3-class win there comes entirely from evicting TRANSIENT tool payloads first while keeping COMMITTED conversation scratch — i.e. the tool-boundary/3-class distinction, NOT shared-prefix pinning” (Committee Q2). The honest blemish is the lone “cap=0.25 + shared_prefix_frac=0.90” corner where “the win IS ~trivial shared-prefix pinning (pin captures 99.9% of the gap)” and “under deep starvation, naive2 slightly out-hits adaptive3” (Verdict PARTIAL). The packet itself grades this “PARTIAL (strongly positive, held-leaning)” and “Candidate-grade for a GPU pass” on devgpu014/H100, explicitly noting “L0 is a sim with content-addressed proxy tokens” and “A live prior-art sweep is still OWED” vs SGLang/RadixAttention, Prompt Cache, CachedAttention, TensorRT-LLM. Without GPU validation and without confirming that existing systems cannot already express the COMMITTED-vs-TRANSIENT classification via priority knobs, the operational win remains a sim microbenchmark, not a production-ready improvement.
```
